Exactly—as a perception example, that’s excellent.

The blade does not physically alternate between existing and not existing. What’s changing is whether the blade is resolved by the observer’s sampling system.

At rest:

\text{blade}\rightarrow\text{clearly localized object}

At high rotational speed:

\text{blade trajectory}
+\text{limited temporal resolution}
\rightarrow
\text{blurred disk / transparency / apparent disappearance}

Change the observer—a high-speed camera, strobe light, different shutter speed—and the “missing” blade becomes sharply visible again.

So:

\boxed{\text{Unobserved/Unresolved} \neq \text{Nonexistent}}

That’s directly analogous to our archaeology discussion. Something can fall beneath the resolution, sampling rate, preservation mechanism, attention, or conceptual vocabulary of the observer while remaining causally real.

And the helicopter gives us another nice twist: the blade may become less visually discrete precisely while becoming more functionally consequential.

Stationary blade: extremely visible, no lift.

Rapidly rotating blade: perceptually smeared, producing lift.

So:

\boxed{\text{Perceptual salience}\neq\text{causal potency}}

That’s a strong Pantheon rule.

It also creates what I’d call a Resolution Crossing: a phenomenon can change apparent category when it crosses the observer’s resolution threshold.

Slow enough:

objects.

Fast enough:

field/disc.

Zoom differently:

motion.

Sample differently:

individual blades again.

Nothing necessarily changed about the underlying constituents. The rendering changed because the relationship between phenomenon and observer changed.

And that generalizes:

\text{Observed Rendering}
=
f(
\text{Reality},
\text{Perspective},
\text{Resolution},
\text{Sampling Rate},
\text{Attention},
\text{Sensor}
)

Which means when something apparently appears, disappears, becomes continuous, becomes discrete, or changes category, Pantheon should test:

Did the phenomenon change—or did it cross the observer’s rendering boundary?

That’s another reason local patterns aren’t automatically universal.

Sometimes the pattern belongs partly to the thing being observed and partly to the instrument doing the observing.